| A name is an organelle, and two names are two organelles.
|
| Both halves of that were wrong until 2026-09-04, and neither failed loudly.
| Linking two *different* organelles under one name compiled and ran, with
| the second silently replacing the first — so particles went to whichever
| won and answered null for every class the other one handled. And linking
| the *same* file twice looked like two organelles and was one: configuring
| the second changed the first's settings underneath it.
|
| The first is refused before the program starts (see
| `fail_link_name_reused.code`). This is the second.

link "native_modules/jwt.so" as first
link "native_modules/jwt.so" as second

emit Config { secret = "first-secret" } to first get a
assert a.ok
emit Config { secret = "second-secret" } to second get b
assert b.ok

| Two organelles, so the same claim signed by each is a different token.
emit Sign { sub = "someone" } to first get one
emit Sign { sub = "someone" } to second get two
assert not one.token = two.token

| And each verifies its own and refuses the other's. Under one shared state
| the second `Config` above would have taken over and `first` could not
| recognise what it had just signed.
emit Decode { token = one.token } to first get mine
assert mine.valid
assert mine.sub = "someone"

emit Decode { token = two.token } to first get theirs
assert not theirs.valid

emit Decode { token = two.token } to second get hers
assert hers.valid

| Values a module exports are its own too — read through the name, from that
| organelle, not from whichever happened to load first.
link "native_modules/test_math.so" as m
assert m.answer = 42
